  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nLatest satellite and radar show some cumulus starting to blossom \nacross the area with scattered showers and thunderstorms to the west \nacross upstate NY. CAMs generally agree that much of the \nthunderstorm activity will remain west of the area as deep layer \nridging holds over eastern Maine. However, instability is \nexpected to build into this evening with SB CAPE climbing above \n2000 J/kg across NH. This may allow for a few storms that form \nto the west to sustain themselves as they cross into NH. Any \nstrong storm that can sustain itself will be capable of gusty \nwinds and torrential rainfall given high PWATs in place, while \nthe threat for severe storms and flash flooding is low. Any \nthunderstorm activity will diminish with the loss of heating \nlater this evening. It will remain warm and humid tonight with \nlows in the upper 60s to low 70s with patchy fog possible across\nmuch of the area, especially in river valleys and near the \ncoast.\n\nHumidity will expand northward Friday as much of the area sees dew \npoints rise into the upper 60s to low 70s. These high dew points \ncombined with temperatures in the upper 80s to low 90s will bring \nheat indices into the mid 90s. I have issued a Heat Advisory across \nSE NH through the coastal plain of Maine where confidence is highest \nfor heat indices greater than 95F. While there will be plenty of \ninstability for thunderstorms the lack of forcing will keep coverage \nisolated at best with much of the area likely seeing mostly sunny \nskies. It will remain warm and humid Friday night with lows in\nthe upper 60s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...Shortwave embedded within warm southwest \nflow aloft will bring a more widespread chance for showers and \nthunderstorms on Saturday. The overall pattern shows modest flow \naloft /H5 winds to 30kts/ with PWATs over 1.75\" given the warm/humid \nairmass in place. There is good agreement through the \nensemble/deterministic suite of measurable rain...but bigger \nquestions remain on how much of the day will be rainy as there is \nthe lack of a defined surface front to organize convection. There \nare a number of model solutions that bring convection in \nearly...which limits the severe threat through the day...but would \nresult in more of a washout with localized downpours and isolated \nflooding possible.  Less morning convection would allow for more \ninstability and a marginal severe weather threat given the \naforementioned moderate wind fields aloft.  Machine learning \nguidance indicates a severe wind threat which correlates well to the \nwind field aloft. At this range...will continue with likely/ \ncategorical PoPs for much of the northern 2/3rds of the forecast \narea /best flow aloft/ with chance to the south. While it will \nremain very muggy...a decent amount of cloud cover will likely keep \ntemperatures cooler than on Friday...with highs generally in the 80s \nthroughout the area. Heat indices will likely still reach 90 south \nof the foothills.   \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3 DESCRIPTION...The week will generally be dominated by \nwest northwest flow aloft with modest ensemble agreement for another \nprecipitation event in the Tuesday/Tuesday night timeframe given a \nfast-moving shortwave embedded within this flow aloft. While there \nare differences in the surface details /particularly given influence \nfrom convection/ a frontal passage appears likely in this timeframe. \nThere would be a conditional severe threat given robust mid level \nflow...but greater questions surround timing and thus instability \npotential.  Will continue to message high chance to likely PoPs for \nthis period and keep an eye on this timeframe for any potential \nsevere / flash flooding threat...though a widespread event does not \nappear likely at this range.  We turn quieter for Wednesday...with \nanother...more substantial cold front arriving to end the \nweek...bringing the potential for a more substantial cool down by \nnext weekend.  For the Tuesday-Thursday period...temperatures should \ngenerally be close to  normal for this time of year...with highs in \nthe 70s to 80s from north to south.",
